<p>Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg testified in a civil jury trial in Los Angeles over whether Instagram and other social platforms were designed in ways that could encourage addictive use among children and harm young people’s mental health. Plaintiffs’ lawyers pressed Zuckerberg on past internal goals and product decisions. He said Meta does not design its apps to “lure” children and argued the company no longer prioritizes maximizing time spent on its services. The exchange underscored a broader global debate about protecting minors online. Zuckerberg said he regretted not moving faster to identify users under 13 and described how difficult reliable age checks can be on the internet. Meta also said device-level age verification would be a better place to enforce age limits, as the case draws attention as a potential test for other lawsuits and regulatory efforts focused on youth social-media harms.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/2z7aiiV0RI_xjbKfLXjCBhp96Nd7BfhEX-dPOmRpRTQe-B5qblCp1mea-DktjTtGbphnUiRkqHZExo44D3eMndJCClgc8sjtSCZSjayPUVCKkaF-VRYeML1zXZtzM1bSm6Y8UGPKh2Tnc2t8DlAHyuVCy7cQzzD2ZS58846AuDy_g4-21zLEIE_HkIak9cTMqNf8CuM-QToYwg-GwrXOTjgdKSpaX4PJngB_N9pxykAkw6mVXW_x5USI7sw9HjGVCqIO8wWGZGzKAh1m4Q' alt='Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg arrives for a landmark trial over whether social media platforms deliberately addict and harm children, Los Angeles, Feb. 18, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Beauty filters: Zuckerberg overruled a group of 18 wellbeing experts who recommended removing beauty filters from Instagram. The feature stayed, despite internal debate about how it might affect young users.</li><li>Engagement goals vs Congress: Plaintiffs pointed to older emails describing ambitions to raise time-spent metrics by double-digit percentage points. Zuckerberg said those were past goals and rejected the claim that they made his 2024 congressional testimony inaccurate.</li><li>Under-13 policy vs internal focus: Zuckerberg repeated that Meta does not allow children under 13 on its platforms. Plaintiffs cited evidence suggesting under-13 users were treated internally as an important audience despite that policy.</li><li>Courtroom milestone: Zuckerberg testified in court for the first time about Instagram’s alleged impact on young users’ mental health.</li></ul><blockquote>If you are trying to say my testimony was not accurate, I strongly disagree with that. - Mark Zuckerberg</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Meta / Mark Zuckerberg: Zuckerberg told jurors Meta’s apps are not designed to lure children and said the company has moved away from product goals aimed at maximizing time spent, pushing back on claims he misled Congress. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5y42znjnjvo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Plaintiffs’ lawyer Mark Lanier: Lanier confronted Zuckerberg with older internal emails and questioned whether Meta’s engagement goals and product choices contradicted later public assurances about not maximizing time spent. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/pixels/article/2026/02/18/zuckerberg-tells-jury-regrets-slow-progress-on-spotting-under-13s-on-instagram_6750629_13.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Meta on enforcement design: Meta’s position emphasized shifting more responsibility for age verification to mobile-device makers, arguing that device-level checks could better support platform age limits. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/metas-zuckerberg-denies-at-la-trial-that-instagram-targets-kids'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/inthenews/comments/1r8mvg4/mark_zuckerberg_tries_to_play_it_safe_in_social/'>Mark Zuckerberg Tries to Play It Safe in Social Media Addiction Trial Testimony</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Justice/2026/0218/zuckerberg-social-media-addiction-trial'>Is Instagram addictive? Mark Zuckerberg faces questions in court.</a> - csmonitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/0c6